//? Title: Geometric hashing: invariant frames, votes and geometric checks
//? Subtitle: Recover a planar similarity, inspect the actual votes and expose quantization failures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A rotated, uniformly scaled and translated point pattern has different coordinates
but the same coordinates relative to a corresponding two-point frame. Geometric
hashing stores those frame coordinates in bins and votes for model bases. Start
with a quotient that can be checked by hand, then follow a tiny actual vote and
recover the original six-point pattern among distractors. The same scene also
exposes a failure: coarser bins can make the highest-vote candidate geometrically
wrong. Native traces distinguish votes, recovered candidates and first-hit inliers;
boundary, reflection, work-count and record-limit examples state the limits of
this particular implementation.
)

\toc

> A two-point frame removes a similarity

Let the ordered basis be b0=(0,0), b1=(2,0), and the query p=(1,1). In the frame
that sends b0 to (0,0) and b1 to (1,0), p has coordinates (0.5,0.5). The perpendicular
axis points counterclockwise from the basis vector. Rotate all three points by a
quarter turn, scale by two and translate by (4,−3): the normalized coordinates
remain (0.5,0.5), up to floating arithmetic. The program computes both frames
through the same native quotient used by the hash builder.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gh_frame.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/geometric_hashing.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> points{{0,0},{2,0},{1,1}};
 const auto transformed=dm::geometry::geometric_transform(points,2,std::numbers::pi/2,{4,-3});
 const auto f=dm::geometry::geometric_hash_frame(points[2],points[0],points[1]);const auto g=dm::geometry::geometric_hash_frame(transformed[2],transformed[0],transformed[1]);
 std::print("| frame | squared basis length | u | v | valid |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| Model | {:.1f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {} |\n| Transformed | {:.1f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {} |\n",f.length_squared,f.u,f.v,f.valid,g.length_squared,g.u,g.v,g.valid);
 std::print("\nTransformed b0 ({:.6f}, {:.6f}); b1 ({:.6f}, {:.6f}); query ({:.6f}, {:.6f}).\n",transformed[0].x,transformed[0].y,transformed[1].x,transformed[1].y,transformed[2].x,transformed[2].y);
 dm::graphics::Diagram d(64,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=4.4;box.height=2.4;box.font_size=22;
 d.node("model",{0,0},"Model points\nb0 = (0, 0), b1 = (2, 0)\np = (1, 1)",box)
 .node("frame",{6,0},std::format("Native frame quotient\nu = {:.1f}\nv = {:.1f}",f.u,f.v),box)
 .node("scene",{12,0},std::format("Transformed points\nb0 = ({:.0f}, {:.0f})\np = ({:.0f}, {:.0f})",transformed[0].x,transformed[0].y,transformed[2].x,transformed[2].y),box)
 .edge("model","frame","divide").edge("scene","frame","divide")
 .text({6,2.5},"Corresponding bases give the same normalized coordinates",24).save_svg("build/gh_frame.svg");
 if(!f.valid||!g.valid||std::abs(f.u-g.u)>1e-14||std::abs(f.v-g.v)>1e-14)throw std::runtime_error("frame invariance");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| frame | squared basis length | u | v | valid |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| Model | 4.0 | 0.500000 | 0.500000 | true |
| Transformed | 16.0 | 0.500000 | 0.500000 | true |

Transformed b0 (4.000000, -3.000000); b1 (4.000000, 1.000000); query (2.000000, -1.000000).
\image(figures/gh_frame.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The basis length changes from 2 to 4, but the dimensionless query coordinates remain 0.5 and 0.5. The same native transform replaces the old report’s local similarity helper.)
\alttext(Three boxes connect original points and transformed points to the native frame quotient u=0.5,v=0.5. The accompanying table prints both actual squared basis lengths and validity flags.)

Treat planar points as complex numbers. With nonzero basis difference d=b1−b0,

$$
w=\frac{p-b_0}{b_1-b_0},\qquad
u=\frac{(p_x-b_{0x})d_x+(p_y-b_{0y})d_y}{d_x^2+d_y^2},\qquad
v=\frac{(p_y-b_{0y})d_x-(p_x-b_{0x})d_y}{d_x^2+d_y^2}.
$$
\alttext(The complex quotient defines u by the dot product with the basis vector and v by the signed cross component, both divided by squared basis length.)

For an orientation-preserving similarity z↦az+t, a≠0, subtracting transformed
origins cancels t, then dividing cancels a. Thus w is invariant in exact arithmetic.
This proof does not cover reflection: complex conjugation sends w to its conjugate,
reversing v. A general affine shear also does not preserve this quotient.

[Lamdan and Wolfson’s original paper](https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~grauman/courses/spring2007/395T/395T/papers/Lamdan88.pdf),
Sections 2.1–2.2, describes the ordered-pair frame, quantized basis records and voting.
Its publication details are recorded by [Tel Aviv University](https://cris.tau.ac.il/en/publications/geometric-hashing-a-general-and-efficient-model-based-recognition/).
The native variant here indexes one model, examines all ordered scene bases and
recovers only the global highest-vote candidate. It does not refine a fit, retry
other candidates after poor verification or match edges. Those differences matter
in the coarse-bin example below.

> Follow actual table insertions and a tiny vote

Use the asymmetric triangle (0,0),(2,0),(0,1). Each of its six ordered bases has
one nonbasis point, so building the table inserts six basis records. A corresponding
scene basis has one point to vote with: a threshold of one is appropriate here;
the default threshold of two would reject this tiny triangle. These are actual
records captured during the executing build and recognition, not recomputed histories.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gh_votes.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/geometric_hashing.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <limits>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> model{{0,0},{2,0},{0,1}};const auto scene=dm::geometry::geometric_transform(model,2,0,{4,-3});
 const auto built=dm::geometry::build_geometric_hash_trace(model,.25,12);const auto t=dm::geometry::recognize_trace(built.run.table,model,scene,0,1,31);
 std::print("| model basis | other point | actual u | actual v | signed bin u | signed bin v |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
 for(const auto&e:built.events)if(e.kind==dm::geometry::GeoHashEventKind::build_frame){const auto b=dm::geometry::geometric_hash_bin(e.u,e.v,.25);std::print("| ({}, {}) | {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {} | {} |\n",e.first,e.second,e.point,e.u,e.v,b.u,b.v);}
 std::print("\n| scene basis | scene point | model basis receiving actual vote | tally after |\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: |\n");
 for(const auto&e:t.events)if(e.kind==dm::geometry::GeoHashEventKind::vote&&e.first==0&&e.second==1)std::print("| ({}, {}) | {} | ({}, {}) | {} |\n",e.first,e.second,e.point,e.model_i,e.model_j,e.count);
 const auto&m=t.run.match;const auto&w=t.run.work;
 std::print("\nTiny recovered candidate {}; votes {}; inliers {}/{}; scale {:.1f}; angle {:.1f}; translation ({:.1f}, {:.1f}); build records {}; recognition records {}; default-threshold found {}.\n",m.found,m.votes,m.inliers,model.size(),m.scale,m.angle,m.translation.x,m.translation.y,built.events.size(),t.events.size(),dm::geometry::recognize(built.run.table,model,scene,0).found);
 auto build=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot({double(built.run.work.frame_attempts),double(built.run.work.insertions)},{"Frames","Inserts"});build.title("Actual triangle build").xlab("").ylab("Primitive operations").y_limits(0,7);
 auto online=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot({double(w.frame_attempts),double(w.bin_lookups),double(w.vote_updates),double(w.candidate_comparisons),double(w.distance_checks)},{"Frames","Lookups","Votes","Tallies","Distances"});online.title("Actual recognition").xlab("").ylab("Primitive operations").y_limits(0,7);
 dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(build).add(online).size(1100,520).title("A three-point model has one nonbasis point per ordered frame").fit_panels().save_svg("build/gh_votes.svg");
 if(!m.found||m.inliers!=3||m.votes!=1||t.events.size()!=31)throw std::runtime_error("tiny votes");
}
